payload.answer:
You're right, change it to `zeroGradient`. There's no special diagnostic reason for pinning it uniformly at the outlet — I just had a mistake in my notes. Use:

```foam
outlet
{
    type            zeroGradient;
}
```

That way the stratified oil–water profile can propagate naturally to the exit without being artificially flattened. Everything else (inlet `alpha.water = 0.3`, fixed mixture velocity at inlet, `noSlip` on the pipe walls) stays as described.

The requirement doesn't pin down specific time values, so this is more of a practical choice. For the 1 m × 1 m dam-break, the relevant time scale is √(L/g) ≈ 0.32 s, so I'd like to run at least far enough to see the bore-front reflect off the right wall.

My suggestion:

```foam
startTime       0;
stopAt          endTime;
endTime         0.1;
deltaT          0.001;
writeControl    timeStep;
writeInterval   10;
purgeWrite      0;
writeFormat     ascii;
writePrecision  6;
```

So `endTime 0.1` s with output every 10 steps (i.e., every 0.01 s) gives about 10 snapshots, which is enough to see the bore propagate and hit the right wall without generating a huge number of directories. `deltaT` of 0.001 s keeps the Courant number reasonable at 1 cm cell size.

If we later want to compare against the Martin & McCutcheon experimental data (which runs to ~0.3 s), we can just bump `endTime` up and maybe tighten `deltaT` to 0.0005. But 0.1 s is a good quick run for the classic setup.

That said, I'm not fussy about the exact numbers — if you'd rather use `endTime 0.2` / `writeInterval 0.005` or something similar, that's totally fine. Just don't go below ~0.1 s or we won't see the reflected wave.
